ture.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Integration with Network Topologies and Infrastructure\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Fiber optic technology and multi-service outlets are often integrated within established network structures. \\\"Topologia em estrela\\\" (Star Topology) serves as a foundational architecture for residential cabling, often incorporating both balanced twisted pair copper cables and coaxial cables for broadband distribution within \\\"Rede residencial\\\" (Residential networks) [Data: Entities (1688, 2308, 2309, 2402), Relationships (3174, 3175, 3180)]. A central distribution point manages these connections, facilitating structured cabling within homes and potentially \\\"Condomínios\\\" (Condominiums) where coaxial is recommended for broadband distribution [Data: Entities (2307, 2310), Relationships (3173, 3190, 3183)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Considerations for High-Power Cable Installations\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The document highlights a specific installation consideration involving \\\"CABOS DE ALTA POTÊNCIA\\\" (High-Power Cables). To prevent interference, it is recommended that fiber optic cables be used in conjunction with high-power cables within shafts, maintaining a separation distance of at least 1.20 meters. This addresses potential signal integrity issues and ensures the reliable operation of both power and data transmission systems in shared conduits [Data: Entities (2112), Relationships (2877)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Challenges and Modularity of Multi-Service Outlets\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Despite their advantages, \\\"TOMADA MULTI-SERVIÇO\\\" (Multi-Service Outlets) present certain challenges. Their integrated design can lead to increased installation complexity compared to individual outlets. Furthermore, a failure in one component might affect other integrated services, and they offer less modularity than separate connec